  "raw_table_text": "Table 1. Baseline characteristics by category of pre-ECMO ventilation duration\nCharacteristic\n0–1 day(n = 787)\n2–3 days(n = 211)\n4–7 days(n = 275)\n8–14 days(n = 115)\n≥ 15 days(n = 32)\nSMD\nMissingn (%)\nAge, years\n57 [49–65]\n58 [48–66]\n59 [52–67]\n59 [55–68]\n62 [54–67]\n0.23\n7 (0.5)\nMale sex\n609 (77.9)\n168 (79.6)\n216 (78.5)\n98 (85.2)\n26 (83.9)\n0.10\n6 (0.4)\nBody mass index, kg/m2\n28.1 [24.7–31.9]\n28.4 [24.6–32.0]\n27.0 [24.3–30.4]\n26.5 [23.9–29.0]\n26.7 [24.5–28.5]\n0.17\n35 (2.5)\nPre-ECMO ventilation, days\n0 [0–1]\n2 [2–3]\n5 [4–6]\n10 [8–12]\n17 [16–21]\n—\n0\nPaO2/FiO2, mmHg\n80 [68–100]\n90 [70–106]\n90 [72–110]\n82 [69–100]\n84 [69–124]\n0.12\n201 (14.2)\nPEEP, cmH2O\n12 [10–15]\n12 [10–15]\n12 [10–15]\n12 [10–14]\n12 [10–15]\n0.14\n198 (13.9)\nPeriod of ECMO initiation\n0.38\n0\nJan–Sep 2020\n112 (14.2)\n53 (25.1)\n70 (25.5)\n26 (22.6)\n6 (18.8)\nOct 2020–Feb 2021\n132 (16.8)\n38 (18.0)\n56 (20.4)\n24 (20.9)\n7 (21.9)\nMar–Jun 2021\n161 (20.5)\n36 (17.1)\n50 (18.2)\n24 (20.9)\n9 (28.1)\nJul–Sep 2021\n227 (28.8)\n65 (30.8)\n84 (30.5)\n33 (28.7)\n9 (28.1)\nOct 2021–Jun 2022\n65 (8.3)\n14 (6.6)\n10 (3.6)\n7 (6.1)\n0 (0)\nJul 2022 onwards\n90 (11.4)\n5 (2.4)\n5 (1.8)\n1 (0.9)\n1 (3.1)\nData are median [interquartile range] for continuous variables and n (%) for categorical variables. Percentages are calculated among patients with non-missing data; the totals for sex do not equal the group sizes because sex was missing in 6 patientsAbbreviations ARDS acute respiratory distress syndrome, ECMO extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, PEEP positive end-expiratory pressure, SMD standardized mean difference\nCharacteristics at ECMO initiation are shown in Table\n1\nand, for the 7-day dichotomy, in Supplementary Table S3. Median age increased modestly across the categories, from 57 to 62 years. Measured hypoxemia at cannulation did not worsen monotonically with ventilation duration; median PaO2/FiO2 ratio was lowest in the 0-1-day category. Cannulation after prolonged ventilation became less frequent later in the pandemic. The proportion of patients ventilated for more than 7 days before cannulation was 12.0% (32 of 267) in the first period and 10.0% (42 of 418) in the period from July to September 2021, but fell to 7.3% (7 of 96) and 2.0% (2 of 102) in the two most recent periods (Supplementary Table S3).\nTransitions out of ECMO\nWithin 90 days after ECMO initiation, 943 patients experienced first liberation, 396 died before first liberation, and 81 remained on ECMO at the end of observation or were lost to follow-up. The cumulative incidence functions are shown in Fig.\n1\nand, for the 7-day dichotomy, in Supplementary Fig. S4. At 90 days, cumulative incidence of first liberation declined from 0.74 in the 0-1-day category to 0.47 in the 8-14-day and at least 15-day categories, whereas that of death before first liberation rose from 0.24 to 0.53 (Table\n3\n).\nFig. 1\nCumulative incidence of first liberation (Panel\nA\n) and of death before first liberation (Panel\nB\n), by category of pre-ECMO ventilation duration. Aalen-Johansen estimates over 90 days from ECMO initiation, treating the two transitions as competing events. Numbers still receiving ECMO at 0, 14, 28, 60, and 90 days are shown beneath the panels. ECMO, extracorporeal membrane oxygenation\nRate of first liberation\nRelative to 0–1 days, the adjusted cause-specific hazard ratio for first liberation over 90 days was 0.87 (95% confidence interval [CI], 0.72–1.05) for 2–3 days, 0.65 (95% CI, 0.54–0.78) for 4–7 days, 0.41 (95% CI, 0.32–0.52) for 8–14 days, and 0.58 (95% CI, 0.34–0.98) for at least 15 days (Table\n2\n, Supplementary Fig. S5). The exposure categories were jointly associated with the liberation rate (chi-square 73.3 on 4 degrees of freedom;\nP\n< 0.001).\n"
